  {unit:'u10s2',q:'Which of the following correctly counts the number of values in a data frame ?',opts:['(A) (using == to detect NA)','(B) (sum of logical TRUE values = count of NA)','(C) (counts non-NA rows)','(D) (built-in R function) \ STATUP'],ans:1,exp:'returns a matrix of logicals. over logicals counts s. Useful NA diagnostics: & Total NAs in entire data frame & NAs per column & NAs per row & Row indices with NA in column x Why (A) fails: always returns (never ) -- so = NA, not a count.'},
  {unit:'u10s2',q:'For a symmetric positive definite matrix , what does return?',opts:['(A) Eigenvalues in ascending order (smallest first)','(B) Eigenvalues in descending order (largest first), all positive for PD matrix','(C) The characteristic polynomial coefficients','(D) The trace and determinant of \ STATUP'],ans:1,exp:'returns a list with: & Eigenvalues (decreasing order for symmetric A) & Eigenvectors as columns (unit vectors) Spectral decomposition: A = QLambda Q^->p where Q= gives PC loadings and variances explained. Hotellings T^2: eigenvalues of HE^-1. Checking PD: all eigenvalues >0 iff PD.'},
  {unit:'u10s2',q:'For a matrix , decomposes it as A = UDV^->p. The rank of equals:',opts:['(A) The number of rows of','(B) The number of non-zero singular values in','(C) The trace of (sum of diagonal)','(D) The determinant of \ STATUP'],ans:1,exp:'returns (U), (D as a vector of singular values sigma_1>=sigma_2>=...>=0), and (V). rank(A) = \#(i: sigma_i > 0) (numerically, >epsilon for machine precision). Applications of SVD: Rank computation & Count non-zero singular values Pseudoinverse & A^+ = V D^-1 U^->p (invert non-zero sigma_i) Low-rank approximation & Keep top k singular values PCA & SVD of centered data matrix Condition number & sigma_1/sigma_min (stability of solution)'},
  {unit:'u10s2',q:'Which R expression is computationally more efficient for computing X^->p X when is a large matrix?',opts:['(A) (explicit transpose then multiply)','(B) (specialised function that exploits symmetry)','(C) Both are equally efficient (identical computation)','(D) (computes XX^->p instead -- same cost) \ STATUP'],ans:1,exp:'Function & Computes & Notes & X^->p X & Faster: uses DSYRK (symmetric rank-k update) & X^->p Y & More efficient than & XX^->p & Symmetric computation & XY^->p & Efficient Why faster? first allocates a new px n matrix for the transpose, then does general matrix-matrix multiplication. avoids the transpose allocation and uses a symmetric BLAS routine (half the operations). For n=10000, p=50: crossprod can be 2-4x faster.'},
  {unit:'u10s2',q:'When writing an R package, the file serves as:',opts:['(A) The file containing all R function code','(B) The metadata file specifying the package name, version, author, dependencies (, ), title, and description','(C) The file that controls which functions are exported to users (public API)','(D) The vignette (documentation) for the package \ STATUP'],ans:1,exp:'File/Directory & Purpose & Metadata: name, version, author, depends & Controls exports/imports (public API) & R function code (.R files) & Documentation (.Rd files) & Long-form tutorials (RMarkdown) NAMESPACE (option C): Controls which functions are exported (visible to users) and which packages are imported.'}
